In Java, you typically handle this by sanitizing inputs before they reach your handler. If you need to validate a parameter from a client, use Apache Commons Validation's `ValidatingInput` or the `ValidatingInputs` interface to enforce strict formatting rules. For HTTP Faucet operations, ensure your adapter passes `headers` and `body` through a `HttpRequestProcessor` that validates them before processing requests.

Here is a minimal example using the `HttpRequestProcessor` pattern:

```java
public class OrderFaucetAdapter {
    public String process(@WebParam(name = "id") String id,
            @WebParam(name = "customerName") String customerName,
            @WebParam(name = "quantity") String quantity,
            @WebParam(name = "status") String status) {
        if (!customerName.isEmpty()) {
            if (!quantity.isEmpty() && !status.contains("available") && status.contains("pending")) {
                throw new ParameterValidationException(
                        "Invalid customer name or quantity required.",
                        "status"
                );
            }
        }
        return execute();
    }

    private String execute() {
        String processed = transport.request().getHeader(HttpRequestProcessor.PROTOCOL_HEADER)
            .getString(HttpRequestProcessor.HEADER_NAME_GETTERS);
        String id = processed.trim();
        String customerName = processed.trim();
        String quantity = processed.trim();
        String status = processed.trim();
        if (id.isEmpty()) {
            throw new ParameterValidationException("Missing customer ID required.", "id");
        }
        return String.format("http://example.com/order/%s/%s/%s", id, customerName, quantity);
    }
}
```

